%% Parallel transport — a short visual lecture: a 3D sphere, the tangent space,
%% and holonomy (a vector that comes back rotated). Build:
%%   lmr2svg examples/parallel-transport/deck.lmr -o parallel-transport.html
!title Parallel Transport

!slide The question

Carry a vector across a surface, always keeping it **parallel to itself** — never
turning it by hand, only sliding it along. Take it once around a closed loop, back
to where it started.

!pause

Does it come back pointing the same way?

!slide Flat space: nothing happens

!anim
	!src flat.py

!slide A curved surface — the tangent space

!anim
	!src tangent.py

!slide The surprise: it comes back rotated

!anim
	!src holonomy.py

!slide Holonomy = the area you enclosed

The turning angle equals the curvature integrated over the enclosed region —
the Gauss–Bonnet theorem:

$ \Delta\theta = \iint_{\Omega} K \, dA $

On the unit sphere ($K = 1$) our triangle has area $\tfrac{\pi}{2}$, so the vector
returns turned by exactly $90^\circ$. Curvature is what a closed loop remembers.
